COS301 · CI/CD Industry Best Practices 4
If you mock the database, you are not integration testing
■ We saw folders called integration_tests/ where every test mocked Prisma, Supabase, or the DB client.
■ That's a controller-routing test. Useful, but it does not exercise SQL, migrations, transactions, RLS, or unique constraints.
■ An integration test boots the real components, end to end inside the process boundary.
■ Industry standard: spin up a real, ephemeral Postgres as the test db, and seed with test data
PATTERN — TESTCONTAINERS (NODE)
import { PostgreSqlContainer } from "@testcontainers/postgresql";
let container, pool;
beforeAll(async () => {
container = await new PostgreSqlContainer("postgres:16").start();
pool = new Pool({ connectionString: container.getConnectionUri() });
await runMigrations(pool);
});
afterAll(async () => { await pool.end(); await container.stop(); });
test("creates and reads a fault", async () => {
const id = await faultsRepo.create(pool, { ref: "F-001" });
expect((await faultsRepo.byId(pool, id)).ref).toBe("F-001");
});

COS301 · CI/CD Industry Best Practices 5
End-to-end means end to end
■ We saw Cypress and Playwright suites where every API call was intercepted and replaced with a hardcoded response.
■ That tests your UI against your imagination of the backend, not the backend.
■ The contract you actually need to verify, auth, status codes, error shapes, is exactly the part you mocked away.
■ Real E2E: a deployed-or-local stack with real services, real DB, real auth. Then drive it from the browser.
USE cy.intercept FOR THIRD-PARTY SERVICES ONLY
// ANTI-PATTERN — every response is invented
cy.intercept('POST', '/api/login', { statusCode: 200, body: { token: 'abc' } });
// CORRECT — only stub the third-party you can't control in CI
cy.intercept('POST', 'https://api.stripe.com/**', { fixture: 'stripe-ok.json' });
cy.visit('/login');
cy.get('[data-test=email]').type('test@uni.ac.za');
cy.get('[data-test=password]').type(Cypress.env('TEST_PW'));
cy.get('[data-test=submit]').click();
cy.url().should('include', '/dashboard'); // your real backend signed it

COS301 · CI/CD Industry Best Practices 6
A test that cannot fail is not a test
■ We saw expect(true).toBe(true), tests with no expect() at all, and assertions on Tailwind class names.
■ We also saw expect(...).not.toThrow without the parentheses, that's a property reference, not a call. It always passes.
■ Assert behaviour, not the implementation. Test what the user sees or what the API returns — never the CSS class soup in
between.
// VACUOUS — passes no matter what
expect(true).toBe(true);
expect(thing).not.toThrow; // missing () — never executes
// BRITTLE — restyle the page, suite goes red
expect(container.querySelector('.shadow-xl')).toBeInTheDocument();
// BEHAVIOURAL — survives a redesign
await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /submit fault/i }));
expect(await screen.findByText(/reference: F-\d+/)).toBeVisible();

COS301 · CI/CD Industry Best Practices 10
Secrets do not belong in your repo
■ We saw plaintext DB passwords in compose files, JWT secrets like supersecret, and the canonical minioadmin:minioadmin
defaults exposed on host ports.
■ Default credentials are scanned for continuously, by Shodan, by GitHub, by every script kiddie.
■ Inject secrets at runtime from a real secret store: GitHub Actions secrets for CI, Vault / AWS Secrets Manager / Doppler for prod.
■ Fail closed. If a required secret is missing, crash the process. Never fall back to a default.
# ANTI-PATTERN — functional weak default; app starts on 'postgres' if env is missing
DB_PASSWORD: ${DB_PASSWORD:-postgres}
# CORRECT — no fallback. Compose interpolation fails the boot.
DB_PASSWORD: ${DB_PASSWORD:?DB_PASSWORD must be set}
# In application code:
const jwtSecret = process.env.JWT_SECRET;
if (!jwtSecret || jwtSecret.length < 32) {
throw new Error("JWT_SECRET missing or too short — refusing to start");
}

ENTITY VS VALUE OBJECT: IDENTITY IS THE TEST.
Ask: 'if I changed every field, would it still be the same thing?' Yes → entity. No → value object.
E N T I T Y
Customer
V A L U E O B J E C T
Money
Has an ID. Two customers with the same name are still different people. Can change email
and remain the same customer.
No ID. R100 ZAR is R100 ZAR — interchangeable. Can't 'change'; you replace it. Equality
compares fields.
class Customer {
readonly id: CustomerId;
email: Email;
status: CustomerStatus;
class Money {
constructor(
readonly amount: Decimal,
readonly currency: Currency,
) {}
changeEmail(e: Email) {
this.email = e;
this.emit(EmailChanged);
}
}
add(other: Money): Money {
return new Money(
this.amount.plus(other.amount),
this.currency);
} }

Breaking the Contract
Changing the contract means everyone depending on it must update. To prevent this,
use versioning when updating services.
// GET /v1/users
{
}
"name": "Alice"
// GET /v2/users
{
}
"fullName": "Alice"
